      <h2 id="common-mistakes">Common mistakes when comparing unit prices</h2>
      <h3>Dividing price by quantity in the wrong order</h3>
      <p>Unit price is always price / quantity, not quantity / price. Dividing the wrong way gives you units per dollar - which sounds plausible but is the inverse. A spoken sanity check catches this: "50 cents per 100 g" makes sense; "2 hundred-grams per dollar" is odd phrasing and signals a division error.</p>
      <h3>Comparing weight units with volume units</h3>
      <p>A 500 ml bottle and a 500 g jar are not the same size for most products. Oil, honey, and sauces are denser than water, so the same volume weighs more. Only compare products that use the same unit type - both by weight or both by volume.</p>
      <h3>Ignoring "per pack" labeling</h3>
      <p>Some shelf labels show "price per 100 g" but the product itself is sold in pre-portioned sachets. A box of 20 sachets × 5 g each is 100 g total - but you're buying 20 servings, and the per-sachet cost may matter more than the per-gram price. Use the count unit for products where individual servings are what you're buying.</p>
      <p>The wrong-direction error (dividing quantity by price) is the one I see most often when helping people set up grocery comparison spreadsheets.</p>
      <p>The result always looks plausible - small decimal number, makes sense as a "rate" - but the moment you try to use it to project a total cost, the math breaks down. If your result gives you something in "grams per dollar," you've divided the wrong way.</p>
      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions about unit price</h2>
      <h3>What is unit price?</h3>
      <p>Unit price is the cost per standard unit of a product - per 100 g, per kg, per lb, or per litre. Divide total price by package quantity to get it. It is the standard metric for comparing differently sized products on the same basis, and it is legally required on retail labels in the EU and many US states.</p>
      <h3>How do you calculate price per kg?</h3>
      <p>Divide the total price by the weight in kilograms. If the weight is in grams, divide by 1,000 first. Example: €4.99 for 750 g = €4.99 / 0.75 kg = €6.65 per kg. This calculator handles the conversion automatically - enter the weight in any unit and it outputs per-kg alongside per-lb.</p>
      <h3>Is a bigger pack always cheaper per unit?</h3>
      <p>Usually, but not always. Sales pricing on smaller sizes, premium brand pricing on large formats, and store-brand vs. name-brand comparisons all create exceptions. The only reliable method is to calculate the unit price for every option before buying - never assume size alone determines value.</p>
      <h3>Can I compare a price per kg with a price per lb?</h3>
      <p>Not directly - 1 kg = 2.205 lb, so the scales are different. Enter one item in kg and the other in lb in this calculator and it converts both to grams internally, then shows results in matching units. You can also compare the per-kg column for both items.</p>
      <h3>What is the difference between unit price and unit rate?</h3>
      <p>Unit price is a specific application - cost per unit of product. <a href="/math/ratio-calculator/unit-rate">Unit rate</a> is the broader mathematical concept: any quantity expressed per one unit, including speed (km per hour), pay ($ per hour), or fuel efficiency (km per litre). Every unit price is a unit rate, but not all unit rates are prices.</p>
